consiSt("wy",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Wyoming Supreme Court","d":"Five justices in Cheyenne, each serving an 8-year term subject to retention elections. Wyoming has no intermediate appellate court, so the Supreme Court hears appeals directly from the district courts and the chancery court. It also decides petitions for a writ of review (interlocutory and extraordinary relief), answers certified questions, admits lawyers, and supervises the Wyoming State Bar."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts in all 23 counties, grouped into nine judicial districts. They hear everything except the civil cases of $50,000 or less, small claims, and misdemeanors that go to the circuit courts. They also sit as appellate courts for circuit courts, municipal courts, and administrative agencies."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Chancery Court","d":"A single statewide court based in Casper for commercial, business, and trust disputes. It hears equitable and declaratory actions and money claims over $50,000 (not counting punitive damages, interest, costs, and fees) arising from listed causes of action, such as breach of contract, breach of fiduciary duty, fraud, trade secrets, UCC transactions, and the internal affairs of business organizations. It holds nonjury trials only. Appeals go directly to the Supreme Court."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"Limited-jurisdiction courts in all 23 counties, with the same nine-district boundaries as the district courts. Exclusive original civil jurisdiction covers claims of $50,000 or less (exclusive of costs), small claims, and forcible entry and detainer. They also hear protection-order cases and all misdemeanors. They have their own civil rules (the W.R.C.P. for Circuit Courts)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Municipal Courts","d":"Hear violations of city and town ordinances. Their judgments are appealed to the district court."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute in force","d":"Wyoming has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting tied to speech or petitioning. Title 1 of the Wyoming Statutes (civil procedure), in the legislature’s compilation dated May 2026, ends at chapter 43 (Mediation). It has no chapter on lawsuits against public participation, and neither ‘SLAPP’ nor ‘public participation’ appears anywhere in it.\nThe Legislative Service Office’s April 20, 2026 memo to the Joint Judiciary Committee says 39 states had anti-SLAPP laws as of April 2026. It names Idaho, Montana, South Dakota, and Utah among the states that adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, and it does not list Wyoming.\nIn a Wyoming court, defend a speech-based suit with the ordinary tools: Rule 12(b)(6), Rule 56, the statutory privileges for fair reports (W.S. 1-29-104, -105), and Rule 11.","c":"Wyo. Stat. tit. 1 (compiled May 2026; chs. 1-43); LSO Memo, Topic Summary: Anti-SLAPP Legislation (Apr. 20, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Watch: anti-SLAPP bill and 2026 interim study","d":"House Bill 103 of the 2026 session (engrossed version), the ‘Wyoming First Amendment Protection Act,’ would have created W.S. 1-44-101 through 1-44-107 (‘Lawsuits Against Public Participation’). The bill provided immunity from suits based on petition, assembly, religious freedom, and speech, and set procedures for such suits. As drafted, it was to take effect July 1, 2026.\nThe 2026 Title 1 compilation contains no chapter 44, and the Joint Judiciary Committee took up anti-SLAPP as a 2026 interim study topic. Together these indicate the bill was not enacted. The bill-status page (a JavaScript app) was not checked, so the final vote was not confirmed.\nA new bill could come in the 2027 general session. Check wyoleg.gov before relying on this entry.","c":"2026 HB0103 (engrossed), 26LSO-0355; LSO Memo (Apr. 20, 2026)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is a two-step process: State Bar certificate first, then a motion by local counsel","d":"Admission pro hac vice is discretionary, and a court is not bound by an earlier admission. For each case, the applicant first applies to the Wyoming State Bar, not the court. The application must include:\n• every jurisdiction where the applicant is licensed;\n• every Wyoming matter in the past 5 years where the applicant sought pro hac vice admission, with the result;\n• all discipline (public or private), disability status, and pro hac vice denials or revocations anywhere, plus anything pending;\n• an acknowledgment that the Wyoming rules apply;\n• the name of local counsel; and\n• a certification under penalty of perjury.\nAttach a certificate of good standing from the highest court of every licensing jurisdiction, dated no more than 30 days before the Bar receives the application. The Bar sets the fee; the rule does not state the amount. Incomplete applications are not accepted.\nThe Bar then issues a ‘Rule 8 certificate.’ Local counsel files it with a motion to admit and local counsel’s own entry of appearance. Admission is denied or revoked if it is being used to get around the admission rules.","c":"Rules Governing the Wyo. State Bar & Authorized Practice of Law R. 8(a)-(d); U.R.D.C. 104(b), (e), (h)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must sign, attend, and answer for the out-of-state lawyer","d":"Local counsel must be an active member of the Wyoming State Bar. Before anything else is filed, local counsel files an entry of appearance and a certificate of compliance with Rule 8.\nLocal counsel must:\n• move the applicant’s admission at the first hearing;\n• sign the first pleading and stay in the case;\n• attend every proceeding unless excused, with full authority to act for the client.\nService on local counsel alone is sufficient notice. Local counsel is deemed to have ratified all of the pro hac vice lawyer’s conduct and is responsible for that lawyer’s ethics violations.\nSeparately, every represented party must have an active Wyoming Bar member at all of its hearings unless the court excuses it.","c":"U.R.D.C. 101(c), 104(e)-(g); Rules Governing the Wyo. State Bar R. 8(e)-(f)","v":true},{"t":"Trial-court admission does not carry over to the Supreme Court","d":"Admission pro hac vice in a trial court does not admit the lawyer before an appellate court. A new motion by local counsel, with Rule 8 compliance, is needed in the Supreme Court.\nUnless excused, appellate local counsel must sign all papers and attend all proceedings. A brief or motion may not carry the name or signature of a lawyer the appellate court has not admitted pro hac vice.","c":"W.R.A.P. 19.01(b)-(e)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: mandatory for Wyoming lawyers, closed to pro hac vice counsel","d":"Statewide trial-court e-filing rules took effect April 5, 2025 and override any local rules. Wyoming-licensed lawyers (and their designated staff) must e-file in courts where e-filing is in place. To register, a filer must complete training and pass an e-filing proficiency exam.\nPro hac vice counsel may not e-file; local counsel e-files for them.\nA document is filed when submitted, and a filing day ends at 11:59:59 p.m. Mountain Time. E-service through the system is service, and the automatic email notices are only courtesies.\nIn the Supreme Court, filings go through the C-Track system (CTEF), and email filing is prohibited.","c":"Wyo. R. Elec. Filing & Serv. 1(b), 4(a)-(c), 5(a)-(b), 7(c); W.R.A.P. 1.01(a), 1.07","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances","d":"Except in criminal cases, a written entry of appearance may be limited to a particular proceeding or matter. An attorney named on a pleading only as having helped prepare it has not appeared.\nSince August 17, 2026, a limited-scope lawyer is deemed withdrawn only after fulfilling the limited duties and filing a notice of conclusion of the limited entry of appearance.\nRPC 1.2(c) treats a client who receives limited representation as unrepresented for purposes of Rules 4.2 and 4.3 unless opposing counsel knows of, or receives, a written notice to communicate only with the limited-scope lawyer.","c":"U.R.D.C. 102(a), (c) (eff. Aug. 17, 2026); Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"Permanent admission: on motion or by UBE transfer","d":"Admission on motion (rules amended effective August 12, 2026) requires:\n• a J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school;\n• admission elsewhere by written exam;\n• at least 300 hours a year of active, authorized practice in 5 of the 7 years before applying;\n• active good standing where the applicant now practices;\n• an NCBE character report.\nUBE transfer is available if the UBE was taken less than 3 years before applying. If it was taken 3-5 years before, the applicant also needs 300 hours a year of practice in the 2 years before applying.\nPassing scores: Legacy UBE 270 or NextGen UBE 620, and MPRE 85. Good-standing certificates for UBE transfer must be dated within 90 days.","c":"Rules & Procedures Governing Admission R. 211(a)-(b), 302, 303, 305 (eff. Aug. 12, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary and in-house practice without pro hac vice","d":"Wyoming’s Rule 5.5(c) follows the ABA temporary-practice safe harbors: work with an actively participating Wyoming lawyer; work related to a proceeding where the lawyer is (or expects to be) authorized; ADR work; and work arising out of the lawyer’s home-state practice.\nUnder Rule 5.5(d), in-house lawyers, including qualifying foreign in-house counsel, may serve their employer. Foreign lawyers must base advice on U.S. or state law on advice from a licensed lawyer. Foreign in-house counsel who are not members of a recognized foreign legal profession need Supreme Court authorization.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)-(e); Rules Governing the Wyo. State Bar R. 7(a)(5)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Wyoming Rules of Professional Conduct for Attorneys at Law","d":"These rules follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and structure, with Wyoming changes. The judiciary’s current compilation is effective September 21, 2026. It includes the July 21, 2026 amendment to Rule 1.15 (safekeeping property and IOLTA, effective September 21, 2026) and the June 17, 2026 amendment to Rule 6.1(b) (effective August 17, 2026).\nRelated rule sets: the Rules Governing Contingent Fees, the Wyoming Rules of Disciplinary Procedure, and the Uniform Rules for District Courts’ standards of professional behavior (U.R.D.C. 801).","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct (compilation eff. Sept. 21, 2026); wyocourts.gov rule-amendments list","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: broader permission to disclose than the ABA rule","d":"Wyoming Rule 1.6(b)(1) lets a lawyer reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary ‘to prevent the client from committing a criminal act.’ That covers any crime, not just the ABA’s reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.\nWyoming also adds (b)(8): disclosure to protect the best interests of an individual when the lawyer is appointed guardian ad litem. The rule protects ‘confidential information,’ a term defined in Rule 1.0.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(a)-(b)","v":true},{"t":"Contingent fees: separate rules, a mandatory form, and presumptive percentages","d":"Rule 1.5(b) requires contingent fee agreements to be in writing and to comply with the separate Rules Governing Contingent Fees. Those rules require:\n• the agreement in duplicate, signed by the attorney and each client;\n• a signed copy delivered to the client, and the lawyer’s copy kept for 3 years;\n• substantially the prescribed form (Attachment I); changes that affect the client’s rights in paragraphs I-XI are void without district-court approval;\n• a copy of the rules given to and fully explained to the client.\nPresumptively reasonable fees on recoveries up to $1,000,000: 33⅓% if settled before suit or within 60 days after filing, and 40% after that or on a verdict. On the excess over $1,000,000, 30% is presumed reasonable. Parties may still contract for other percentages.\nA client may ask the State Bar fee-dispute committee to review the fee within 60 days after the final distribution of proceeds.\nNo contingent fee is allowed in criminal or divorce matters. No one may take a fee merely for a referral.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)-(d); Rules Governing Contingent Fees R. 3-6","v":true},{"t":"Rule 8.4(g) is different: no anti-harassment rule; a ban on using disbarred lawyers","d":"Wyoming’s Rule 8.4 does not include the ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) ban on harassment and discrimination. Wyoming’s 8.4(g) instead makes it misconduct to knowingly employ or contract with anyone disbarred, suspended, or on incapacity or disability-inactive status in any jurisdiction, in any law-related role (employee, contractor, paralegal, secretary, investigator, or consultant), paid or unpaid.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(g)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.15A: client-file rule with a 5-year retention period","d":"Wyoming adds Rule 1.15A, which has no ABA counterpart. It defines the client file and requires the lawyer to provide it to the client or former client on request, in commonly available electronic or paper form. The lawyer may withhold documents the client has not paid for under the fee agreement.\nUnless the file is transferred or the client agrees in writing to another arrangement, the lawyer must keep it at least 5 years after the matter ends. Files involving a minor have a longer period.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.15A(a)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Pro bono: 50-hour goal, or a $500 contribution","d":"Like the ABA rule, each lawyer should aspire to 50 hours of pro bono service a year, with a substantial majority in Wyoming and for persons of limited means. Wyoming’s Rule 6.1(b), amended effective August 17, 2026, offers an alternative: a voluntary $500 yearly contribution to a nonprofit that provides direct legal help to persons of limited means. Examples given are the Wyoming Foundation for Civil Justice, Legal Aid of Wyoming, and the UW College of Law clinics.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 6.1 (eff. Aug. 17, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Guardian-ad-litem role written into the rules","d":"Rule 1.2(e): a lawyer appointed guardian ad litem represents what the lawyer reasonably believes is in the individual’s best interests and is not bound by the individual’s objectives, but must consult with the individual in an age- and ability-appropriate way.","c":"Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(e), 1.6(b)(8)","v":true}],"style":"State of Wyoming","home":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Peremptory strike of the judge: plaintiff gets 14 days; defendant must use it by the first response","d":"Out-of-state lawyers often expect no peremptory challenge, or a longer window. In Wyoming a plaintiff must move within 14 days after the clerk’s notice of assignment (or of a reassignment). A defendant must move by its first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion, or 14 days after a reassignment, whichever is later. A party gets one strike per case, and parties added later get none.","c":"W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(1)"},{"t":"Post-judgment motions are deemed denied at 90 days, and the appeal clock starts then","d":"A Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) motion not decided within 90 days after filing is deemed denied, unless the court enters an order continuing it, for no more than 60 days. The 30 days to appeal then run from the deemed denial, not from any later ruling. A judge who rules after the deemed-denial date has lost jurisdiction to decide the motion.","c":"W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4); W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 2.02(b)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: file in the trial court, serve the Supreme Court clerk, and attach the appendix list","d":"The notice of appeal is filed with the trial-court clerk within 30 days of entry of the appealable order, and that deadline is jurisdictional. A copy must also go to the appellate clerk at the same time.\nIn civil cases the notice needs an appendix listing (not attaching) every pleading asserting a claim, every order disposing of claims or parties, and the judgment and any decision letter. The notice must also carry a certificate about ordering the transcript.\nMissing pieces bring monetary sanctions when the appeal is docketed. The docket fee is paid to the trial-court clerk when the notice is filed.","c":"W.R.A.P. 1.03, 2.01(a), 2.05(a), 2.07, 2.09(a)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice does not travel, and you cannot e-file","d":"Trial-court pro hac vice admission does not admit you in the Supreme Court; a new motion is required. Every admission requires a separate Wyoming State Bar application and Rule 8 certificate for each case. Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file in the trial courts, so local counsel files everything.","c":"W.R.A.P. 19.01(c); Rules Governing the Wyo. State Bar R. 8(c)-(d); Wyo. R. Elec. Filing & Serv. 4(b)(1)"},{"t":"A Wyoming lawyer must attend every hearing","d":"An active Wyoming Bar member must attend all hearings of any represented party unless the court excuses it after notice to other counsel. For pro hac vice matters, local counsel must be present at all proceedings, with full authority. Budget for local counsel at every setting, including pretrial conferences.","c":"U.R.D.C. 101(c), 104(f)(3)"},{"t":"Counsel cannot stipulate to extensions or continuances","d":"Unlike common practice elsewhere, Wyoming counsel cannot extend rule- or order-based deadlines by agreement. Only a court order does it, except where the W.R.C.P. allow otherwise. One exception: a timely, one-time clerk’s extension of up to 15 days to answer, or to respond to interrogatories, document requests, or requests for admission.","c":"U.R.D.C. 201, 202; W.R.C.P. 6(b)(3)"},{"t":"No discovery until initial disclosures are done, and there is no automatic Rule 26(f) conference","d":"Initial disclosures are due within 30 days after the answer is due. No party may seek discovery from any source until that period has run and the party has made its own disclosures. Wyoming has no mandatory early meet-and-confer; a discovery conference happens only if the court orders it or a party moves for one.","c":"W.R.C.P. 26(a)(1.3), 26(d)(1), 26(f)"},{"t":"Voluntary dismissal no longer gets the savings statute (since September 21, 2026)","d":"Under amended Rule 41, a voluntary dismissal is not a ‘failure otherwise than upon the merits’ under W.S. 1-3-118. Do not dismiss voluntarily and plan to refile under the savings statute after limitations has run.","c":"W.R.C.P. 41(a)(3) (eff. Sept. 21, 2026)"},{"t":"Contingent fee agreements must use Wyoming’s form","d":"A standard engagement letter from another state will not comply. Wyoming requires an agreement in duplicate, signed by the lawyer and each client, substantially in the prescribed form, with a copy of the contingent fee rules given to and explained to the client. Changes that affect the client’s rights need district-court approval. Each fee-sharing lawyer is liable for the others’ malpractice as if partners.","c":"Rules Governing Contingent Fees R. 4, 5(d)-(e), 6; Wyo. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)"},{"t":"Costs and fees: two short clocks after judgment","d":"Under W.R.C.P. 54(d), costs other than attorney’s fees and claims for attorney’s fees both require a motion within 21 days after entry of judgment. U.R.D.C. 501(a) also requires an itemized certificate of costs within 20 days after entry of a final judgment allowing costs, and objections are due within 10 days. File by the earlier date and satisfy both.\nU.R.D.C. 501(a)(3)(B) limits taxable witness fees to $30 a day, plus mileage at $.23 a mile. Expert fees are limited to $25 a day unless the court allows more.","c":"W.R.C.P. 54(d)(1)-(2); U.R.D.C. 501(a)"},{"t":"Answer in 20 days and serve within 90","d":"A defendant served in Wyoming answers within 20 days, or 30 days if served outside Wyoming or by publication. A plaintiff who has not served a defendant within 90 days after filing faces dismissal without prejudice under W.R.C.P. 4(w) and U.R.D.C. 203(b).","c":"W.R.C.P. 4(w), 12(a)(1)(A); U.R.D.C. 203(b)"},{"t":"Chancery court means no jury","d":"Removing a business case to chancery court (or filing there) waives a jury, because the court uses only nonjury trials. Removal from district court requires every party’s written consent within 60 days after service on the last defendant.","c":"W.S. 5-13-115(a); W.R.C.P. 3.2(a)"}],"judge":[{"t":"One peremptory disqualification per party, on a short clock","d":"In district court, a party may disqualify the assigned judge without cause by motion, once per case and against one judge only. The clerk must enter a notice of assignment within 5 days after the complaint is filed.\nDeadlines:\n• Plaintiff: 14 days after that notice, or 14 days after an order reassigning the case, whichever is later.\n• Defendant: by the time of its first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion, or 14 days after a reassignment, whichever is later.\nParties added after the first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion have no peremptory challenge. New counterclaims, crossclaims, or later motions do not revive it. The rule does not apply in criminal or juvenile cases.","c":"W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(1)(A)-(H)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause","d":"A party may move for a change of judge, supported by affidavits, whenever the grounds become known. The grounds are that the judge:\n• was counsel in the action before becoming a judge;\n• is interested in the action;\n• is related by blood to a party;\n• is a material witness; or\n• is biased or prejudiced against the party or counsel.\nOther parties may file counter-affidavits. The presiding judge hears the motion or may refer it to another judge.\nA ruling on a change of judge is not an appealable order. It can be raised only as error on appeal from the final judgment.","c":"W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(2)-(4)","v":true},{"t":"Transfer of trial to another county","d":"Separate from change of judge: any party may move to transfer trial within 15 days after the last pleading, for local prejudice or witness convenience. The court sends the case to the most convenient county to which no party’s objection applies. The moving party pays the transmission costs and the new county’s docket fee within 14 days.","c":"W.R.C.P. 40.1(a)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Statutory fair-report privileges","d":"A fair and impartial report of proceedings before state or municipal legislative or executive bodies, or of documents presented in them, is privileged unless published maliciously.\nA fair report of an indictment, warrant, arrest, or any civil or criminal court filing is privileged unless it was published maliciously, or the publisher refused to publish the plaintiff’s reasonable explanation or contradiction, or refused to publish the later outcome of the suit on request.\nBroadcasters are not liable for defamation by non-employees unless they failed to use due care. They are not liable at all for statements by political candidates. Broadcast defamation plaintiffs recover only actual damages alleged and proved.","c":"W.S. 1-29-101 to 1-29-105","v":true},{"t":"State constitutional speech protection: not checked","d":"The Wyoming Constitution’s speech and press guarantees and any case law reading them more broadly than the First Amendment are not in the collected texts and were not checked.","c":"Wyo. Const. art. 1 (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Chancery court: Wyoming’s business court, with no jury","d":"The chancery court is a statewide court of limited jurisdiction for commercial, business, and trust disputes. It uses nonjury trials, ADR, and limited motion practice.\nA district-court case can move there only with every party’s written consent:\n• within 60 days after service on the last defendant; or\n• within 60 days after a paper first shows the case is removable.\nThe case must also meet the W.S. 5-13-115 eligibility requirements. District-court filing fees are not refunded.","c":"W.S. 5-13-115(a)-(b); W.R.C.P. 3.2","v":true},{"t":"Uniform Rules for District Courts: no stipulated continuances or extensions","d":"Cases are not continued on counsel’s stipulation; a continuance requires written good cause. Time limits set by rule or order cannot be extended by agreement of counsel, only by court order (except as the W.R.C.P. allows).\nThe court will dismiss a case on file 90 days without service on the defendant unless extended for good cause before then. Cases with no substantial action of record for 90 days are subject to dismissal for lack of prosecution.","c":"U.R.D.C. 201, 202, 203(b)-(c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Wyoming Rules of Appellate Procedure (W.R.A.P.), judiciary compilation “WRAP-eff-Dec-1” including the December 11, 2024 amendments (eff. March 1, 2025) and the November 18, 2025 amendments to Rules 3.02 and 3.09 (eff. January 19, 2026). Wyoming Rules of Civil Procedure (W.R.C.P.), compilation effective September 21, 2026, Rules 6, 50, 52, 54, 59, 60. Rules of the Supreme Court of Wyoming (May 2022 compilation), Rule 3. Wyoming Rules for Electronic Filing and Service (eff. September 2026). Official texts from wyocourts.gov; plain-English summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Wyoming: one appellate court, a jurisdictional 30-day notice, and deemed-denied post-trial motions","d":"Wyoming has no intermediate appellate court. Final judgments and appealable orders of the district courts and the chancery court go straight to the Wyoming Supreme Court. District courts sit as appellate courts for circuit courts, municipal courts, and agencies; their final judgments can then be appealed to the Supreme Court.\nOnly one thing is jurisdictional: filing a compliant notice of appeal in the trial court within 30 days of entry of the appealable order. Every other rule violation leads to sanctions the court considers appropriate, which can include dismissal or summary affirmance.\nThe two biggest traps for lawyers from other states:\n• the deemed-denial rule for post-trial motions (W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4)), which starts the appeal clock without any order; and\n• the petition for a writ of review, which must be filed within 15 days for interlocutory relief.","c":"W.R.A.P. 1.03(a), 1.04, 2.01, 2.02, 13.03; W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4)"},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final judgments and ‘appealable orders’","d":"Appealable orders are:\n• an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment;\n• an order affecting a substantial right made in a special proceeding;\n• an order on a summary application after judgment;\n• an order (including a conditional one) granting a new trial under W.R.C.P. 59(a)(4) or (5); and\n• interlocutory orders granting, continuing, modifying, dissolving, or refusing to dissolve or modify injunctions, or appointing receivers or winding up receiverships.\nAll other interlocutory review goes through a writ of review (Rule 13).","c":"W.R.A.P. 1.05"},{"t":"Partial judgments need Rule 54(b) certification","d":"When multiple claims or parties are involved, an order resolving fewer than all of them is final only if the court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay (W.R.C.P. 54(b)). Without that, the order can be reviewed only by writ of review or after final judgment. A decision letter is not a judgment (W.R.C.P. 54(a)).","c":"W.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b); W.R.A.P. 13.02"},{"t":"Change-of-judge rulings wait for the final appeal","d":"A ruling on a motion to change the district judge, whether peremptory or for cause, is not an appealable order. It is entered on the docket and may be raised as error on appeal from the final judgment.","c":"W.R.C.P. 40.1(b)(3)"}]],["Notice of appeal",[{"t":"30 days, filed in the trial court, copy to the appellate clerk","d":"File the notice with the trial-court clerk within 30 days from entry of the appealable order, and serve it under W.R.C.P. 5. At the same time, serve a copy on the clerk of the appellate court. In criminal cases, also serve the public defender and the attorney general, and serve any guardian ad litem.\nTimely filing of a notice that complies with Rule 2.07(a) is jurisdictional.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 1.03(a)"},{"t":"Contents and civil appendix","d":"The notice must:\n• name the appealing parties;\n• identify the judgment or order (or the portion) appealed;\n• name the court appealed to;\n• carry the transcript certificate required by Rule 2.05(a); and\n• show which counsel represents which parties when there are several.\nIn civil cases, add an appendix that lists (does not attach):\n• all pleadings asserting claims or adding or removing parties;\n• all orders disposing of claims or parties; and\n• the final judgment and any decision letter.\nA missing appendix or a missing copy to the appellate clerk can bring a monetary sanction.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.07, 1.03(b)(1)"},{"t":"Fees at filing","d":"When filing the notice, deliver to the trial-court clerk the appellate docket fee and the trial clerk’s fee for preparing the record, or file a motion to proceed in forma pauperis. The Supreme Court’s docket fee for an appeal or any original proceeding, including writs of review and certifications, is $140 (Rules of the Supreme Court of Wyoming, May 2022 compilation).\nIn civil cases, IFP status on appeal is available only when a statute or constitutional right allows it. If IFP is denied, pay the docket fee within 30 days of the order or the appeal goes no further.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.09(a)-(c); Wyo. Sup. Ct. R. 3(a)"},{"t":"Extension for excusable neglect: apply before day 45","d":"The trial court may extend the time to appeal for excusable neglect, but the application must be filed before 45 days from entry of the order have passed. File with it a proposed notice of appeal and the docket fee (or an IFP motion). If the court does not grant the extension within 14 days after the application is filed, it is deemed denied.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Once a timely notice is filed, any other party may file its own notice within the original 30 days or within 15 days after the first notice was filed. A separate notice is needed for each order you challenge. Amended notices may correct only clerical errors, or bring up a later order amending the one appealed or an order on a pending post-trial motion.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(2), (c)"},{"t":"Premature notices","d":"A notice filed after the court announces a decision but before the judgment or order is entered is treated as filed on, and after, the date of entry.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.04"}]],["Post-trial motions that stop the clock",[{"t":"Which motions toll","d":"The time to appeal stops running for all parties when a party timely files one of these:\n• a Rule 50(b) motion for judgment;\n• a Rule 52(b) motion to amend or make additional findings;\n• a Rule 59 motion to alter or amend; or\n• a Rule 59 motion for a new trial.\nThe chancery-court equivalents also toll. Each motion is due 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court cannot extend that time.\nA bare ‘motion to reconsider’ is not on the list, and the annotated cases treat such motions as not tolling the appeal time.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.02(a); W.R.C.P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)"},{"t":"Deemed denial at 90 days","d":"A motion under Rule 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) that is not decided within 90 days after filing is deemed denied. The only exception is an order, entered within that period, continuing the determination for no more than 60 days.\nThe full 30-day appeal time runs from the order granting or denying the motion or, if no order is entered, from the deemed denial.","c":"W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4); W.R.A.P. 2.02(b)"},{"t":"Notice filed while a motion is pending","d":"A notice of appeal filed after judgment but before the last tolling motion is resolved takes effect when the final order disposing of that motion is entered, or when the motion is deemed denied. To challenge the ruling on the motion itself, file an amended notice within the Rule 2.01 time; no extra fee is required.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.02(c)"}]],["Record and transcript",[{"t":"Order the transcript with the notice","d":"When filing the notice, order the transcript and arrange payment with the court reporter, or file an IFP motion. Certify this on the notice. If no transcript will be ordered, say so and say whether you will seek a statement of evidence (Rule 3.03) or an agreed statement (Rule 3.08).\nIn civil cases, also file and serve a description of the transcript parts you are ordering and, unless ordering the whole transcript, a statement of the issues. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported by the evidence, include all the evidence relevant to that finding. The appellee may order more parts within 15 days.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.05(a), 3.02(b) (eff. Jan. 19, 2026)"},{"t":"Reporter’s deadline","d":"The court reporter files the transcript in the trial court within 60 days after the notice of appeal. The trial court may extend that for good cause on a specific motion. The reporter then notifies the appellate court and parties. Condensed transcripts are not allowed in the Supreme Court.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.06, 3.02(d)"},{"t":"Designate the record with your brief","d":"The appellant files a designation of the record parts it relies on in the trial court at the same time as filing its brief. The appellee designates any additions with its brief, and the appellant may add more by the reply deadline.\nIn civil appeals, do not designate the entire record without an appellate court order, and leave out discovery papers, subpoenas, and setting notices unless they are relevant. Failure to designate can bring sanctions. Cite the record by the trial clerk’s page (Bates) numbers, without volume numbers.","c":"W.R.A.P. 3.05(b)-(e), 7.01(f), 1.03(b)(2)"},{"t":"Docketing and jurisdiction","d":"The appeal is docketed when the trial clerk certifies that the record is complete and forwards the docket fee or IFP order. The appellate clerk then serves a docketing notice that sets the briefing schedule. The appellate court acquires jurisdiction on docketing. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over matters not on appeal, including bonds and stays.","c":"W.R.A.P. 6.01(a)-(b)"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"Supersedeas bond set by the trial court","d":"To stay execution, present a supersedeas bond to the trial court. For an unsecured money judgment, the bond covers the unsatisfied judgment, costs on appeal, and interest, unless the court, after notice and hearing and for good cause, sets another amount or other security. Executors, administrators, and guardians must give a bond.\nUnder W.R.C.P. 62(a), the automatic stay of execution lasts only 14 days after judgment.","c":"W.R.A.P. 4.02; W.R.C.P. 62(a)"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Schedule: 45 / 45 / 15","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 45 days after service of the docketing notice. The appellee’s brief is due 45 days after service of the appellant’s brief. An optional reply is due 15 days after service of the appellee’s brief.\nThe court may shorten the schedule. In termination of parental rights, adoption, abuse and neglect, delinquency, and CHINS cases, the court will not entertain extension motions.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.06"},{"t":"Extensions are hard to get","d":"Only the appellate court can extend briefing time, on a motion certifying good cause filed before the deadline and at least 3 working days before the due date. A later motion must explain why it could not be filed earlier.\nGood cause means things like a death in counsel’s immediate family or serious illness. ‘Counsel is too busy’ is not enough. Absent extraordinary circumstances, the court will not consider extensions for reply briefs.\nIf the appellant fails to file a brief, the appeal is dismissed for want of prosecution. If the appellee fails to file, the appellee is not heard.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.10, 7.11"},{"t":"Page limits and format","d":"Principal briefs may not exceed 60 pages and reply briefs 18 pages, excluding the title page, tables, certificate of service, and appendices. Amicus briefs are limited to 35 pages.\nFormat: 8½ x 11 pages, 1-inch margins, double spacing (except quotations over 50 words), and 13-point minimum type, with footnotes in the same size. Bind only at the upper left corner.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.05, 7.12(d)"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"The appellant’s brief contains, in order:\n• a title page (with the Wyoming Bar number);\n• a table of contents and a table of authorities;\n• a jurisdictional statement covering finality and timeliness;\n• the issues and the statement of the case, with record cites;\n• the argument, with a standard of review for each issue;\n• a conclusion stating the precise relief sought;\n• signature and a certificate of service; and\n• an appendix with the judgment, the decision letter or reasons, and the Rule 10.01 statement of costs.\nThe reply is limited to new issues raised by the appellee and must list them on its first page. Arguments without cogent reasoning or authority may be summarily rejected.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.01, 7.02, 7.03, 1.03(a)"},{"t":"Amicus curiae","d":"Amicus briefs are allowed only by leave of court. The motion, with the proposed brief attached, is due within 11 days after the principal brief of the party supported is filed, or within 11 days after the first party brief for an amicus supporting neither side. Amici may not file reply briefs, and they may argue only with the court’s permission and the supported party’s consent.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.12"}]],["Argument, decision, rehearing, mandate",[{"t":"Brief-only docket or oral argument","d":"The Supreme Court assigns each case to either the brief-only docket (submitted without argument) or the oral argument docket. A party may move, with good cause, within 15 days after a brief-only assignment to have the case reassigned for argument.\nEach side gets 30 minutes unless the court orders otherwise. A motion for more time is due at least 15 days before argument. Argument by videoconference is allowed only in exceptional circumstances, on a motion filed at least 20 days before argument.","c":"W.R.A.P. 8.01, 8.02"},{"t":"Rehearing: 15 days","d":"A petition for rehearing, with a supporting brief (they may be combined), is due no later than 15 days after the decision and must be served within that time. There is no oral argument unless the court requests it. A timely petition suspends proceedings under the decision. If rehearing is granted, the other party answers within 15 days.","c":"W.R.A.P. 9.08-9.10"},{"t":"Mandate and costs","d":"The mandate issues when rehearing is denied, or if no petition or other motion is filed within 15 days after the decision. Motions for costs or fees are due within 15 days after the final opinion or order is filed. The court cannot hear a costs motion after the mandate issues.\nIf the court certifies there was no reasonable cause for the appeal, it may award the appellee attorney’s fees of $100 to $10,000 plus up to $2,000 in damages. It may tax them against counsel rather than the client for deficient briefs or frivolous or dilatory appeals.","c":"W.R.A.P. 9.11, 10.05(b)-(c), 10.06"},{"t":"Further review","d":"The Supreme Court’s decision is final on questions of state law. Only federal questions can go further, by certiorari to the U.S. Supreme Court.","c":"Wyoming Judicial Branch, Supreme Court page (wyocourts.gov)"}]],["Interlocutory review and original writs",[{"t":"Petition for writ of review: 15 days","d":"Interlocutory or extraordinary relief from a district court or chancery court order is sought in the Supreme Court by a petition for a writ of review. Habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, and quo warranto are treated as writs of review.\nThe petition is due within 15 days after entry of the order, with the docket fee. Responses are due within 15 days after filing. The court may grant the petition after day 30 from the order, or once both papers are filed. If review is not accepted within 40 days after the petition is filed, it is deemed denied.\nThe petition is filed on paper; responses are e-filed. Filing the petition does not stay the lower court unless a court orders a stay.","c":"W.R.A.P. 13.01, 13.03, 13.06, 1.01(a)(4)"},{"t":"Standard for interlocutory review","d":"A writ of review may issue for an interlocutory order that is not otherwise appealable but involves a controlling question of law with substantial grounds for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation. Granting it is discretionary.","c":"W.R.A.P. 13.02, 13.01(a)"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"The Supreme Court may answer questions of law certified by a federal court, the chancery court, or a district court. The question must be potentially determinative, with no controlling Wyoming Supreme Court precedent. Any party may move for certification. No mandate issues after the answer.","c":"W.R.A.P. 11.01, 11.02, 9.07"}]],["Review of agency action",[{"t":"Petition for review in district court: 30 days","d":"Judicial review of agency action starts with a petition for review in the district court with venue. The petition is due within 30 days after service of the final agency decision, or of the denial of rehearing or the decision after rehearing, even if a statute allows a different time. No summons is needed.\nFor excusable neglect, the district court may extend the time by up to 30 days. Cross-petitions are due within 15 days after the first petition. Order the transcript when you file.","c":"W.R.A.P. 12.03, 12.04"},{"t":"Certification straight to the Supreme Court","d":"Within 30 days after the petition is filed, any party may move to have the district court certify the case to the Supreme Court (responses are due in 15 days). Grounds include a novel or constitutional question, statewide impact, or a likely appeal anyway.\nThe Supreme Court accepts or rejects a certified case within 30 days after receiving the order. The district court’s final judgment on review can be appealed to the Supreme Court.","c":"W.R.A.P. 12.09(b)-(d), 12.11"}]],["Filing, service, and time",[{"t":"E-filing in the Supreme Court","d":"Briefs, motions, and other papers are e-filed through the C-Track Electronic Filing System (CTEF), and the electronic version is the official filing. Until otherwise ordered, also file paper copies: the original and 6 copies in the Supreme Court, or the original and 2 in the district court. District-court filings need a proposed order; Supreme Court filings must not include one.\nEmail filing is prohibited. Nonconforming documents are pulled from the docket and must be refiled within the time given, or they are untimely.","c":"W.R.A.P. 1.01, 1.07"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the day of the event and include the last day. If the last day is a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed by weather, the deadline moves to the next business day.\nAdd 3 days when a paper is served by mail. Nothing is added for service through the court’s e-filing system, or when the period runs from a filing or a fixed date.","c":"W.R.A.P. 14.02, 14.03"},{"t":"Motions in the appellate court","d":"A motion that may substantially affect the disposition must be supported by a memorandum of points and authorities. The opposing party may respond within 15 days after service. The court may decide motions without argument.","c":"W.R.A.P. 16(b)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil or criminal)","d":"30 days from entry of the appealable order or judgment.\nJurisdictional. File with the trial-court clerk, and serve a copy on the appellate clerk at the same time. On a showing of excusable neglect the trial court may extend the time, but only if the application is filed within 45 days of entry of the appealable order (W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)).","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 1.03(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the appealable order or judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. File with the trial-court clerk, and serve a copy on the appellate clerk at the same time. On a showing of excusable neglect the trial court may extend the time, but only if the application is filed within 45 days of entry of the appealable order (W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1))."}},{"t":"Application to extend time to appeal (excusable neglect)","d":"45 days from entry of the appealable order.\nMust be filed before day 45, with a proposed notice and the docket fee or an IFP motion. Deemed denied if not granted within 14 days of filing.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the appealable order)","note":"Must be filed before day 45, with a proposed notice and the docket fee or an IFP motion. Deemed denied if not granted within 14 days of filing."}},{"t":"Trial court order on extension application (else deemed denied)","d":"14 days from filing of the extension application.\nIf denied or deemed denied, the docket fee is refunded.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the extension application)","note":"If denied or deemed denied, the docket fee is refunded."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal / other party’s notice of appeal","d":"15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(2)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"Or within the original 30 days, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Tolling motion: Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59 new trial or alter/amend","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nThe court cannot extend this time (W.R.C.P. 6(b)(2)).","c":"W.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); W.R.A.P. 2.02(a)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"The court cannot extend this time (W.R.C.P. 6(b)(2))."}},{"t":"Post-trial motion deemed denied","d":"90 days from filing of the Rule 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59 or 60(b) motion.\nUnless continued by order within the 90 days, for no more than 60 more days. The 30-day appeal time then runs from the deemed denial.","c":"W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Rule 50(b),)","note":"Unless continued by order within the 90 days, for no more than 60 more days. The 30-day appeal time then runs from the deemed denial."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after tolling motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order deciding the last tolling motion, or its deemed denial.\nA notice filed earlier takes effect on disposition. To challenge the ruling on the motion, file an amended notice within this time.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.02(b), 2.01(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order deciding the last tolling motion, or its deemed denial)","note":"A notice filed earlier takes effect on disposition. To challenge the ruling on the motion, file an amended notice within this time."}},{"t":"Pay docket fee after IFP denial","d":"30 days from entry of the order denying in forma pauperis status.\nIf the fee is not paid, the appeal does not proceed.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.09(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying in forma pauperis status)","note":"If the fee is not paid, the appeal does not proceed."}},{"t":"Appellee orders additional transcript (civil)","d":"15 days from service of appellant’s partial-transcript designation.\nOr obtain a trial-court order directing the appellant to order those parts.","c":"W.R.A.P. 3.02(b)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s partial-transcript designation)","note":"Or obtain a trial-court order directing the appellant to order those parts."}},{"t":"Court reporter files transcript","d":"60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nThe trial court may extend it for good cause on a specific motion.","c":"W.R.A.P. 2.06(a)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"The trial court may extend it for good cause on a specific motion."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"45 days from service of the notice that the appeal is docketed.\nFile the record designation in the trial court at the same time (W.R.A.P. 3.05(b)).","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.06(b)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice that the appeal is docketed)","note":"File the record designation in the trial court at the same time (W.R.A.P. 3.05(b))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"45 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nFile any additional record designation at the same time.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.06(c)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"File any additional record designation at the same time."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"15 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nLimited to new issues. Extensions are not considered absent extraordinary circumstances.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.06(d), 7.03","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Limited to new issues. Extensions are not considered absent extraordinary circumstances."}},{"t":"Motion to extend briefing time","d":"3 days from before the brief’s due date (working days).\nMust certify good cause. A later motion must explain the delay.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.10(a)","due":{"n":3,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Must certify good cause. A later motion must explain the delay."}},{"t":"Amicus motion with proposed brief","d":"11 days from filing of the principal brief of the party supported.\nAn amicus supporting neither side: 11 days after the first party brief.","c":"W.R.A.P. 7.12(e)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the principal brief of the party supported)","note":"An amicus supporting neither side: 11 days after the first party brief."}},{"t":"Motion to move case from brief-only docket to argument","d":"15 days from entry of the order assigning the brief-only docket.\nRequires good cause and is discretionary.","c":"W.R.A.P. 8.01(c)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order assigning the brief-only docket)","note":"Requires good cause and is discretionary."}},{"t":"Motion for extra oral argument time","d":"15 days from before oral argument.\nDefault is 30 minutes per side.","c":"W.R.A.P. 8.02(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (before oral argument)","note":"Default is 30 minutes per side."}},{"t":"Motion for oral argument by videoconference","d":"20 days from before the scheduled argument date.\nExceptional circumstances only. Later motions are allowed only for weather or unforeseen events.","c":"W.R.A.P. 8.01(e)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (before the scheduled argument date)","note":"Exceptional circumstances only. Later motions are allowed only for weather or unforeseen events."}},{"t":"Response to an appellate motion","d":"15 days from service of the motion.\nApplies to motions that may substantially affect disposition.","c":"W.R.A.P. 16(b)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Applies to motions that may substantially affect disposition."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"15 days from date the decision is rendered.\nFile and serve within the period. Suspends proceedings (W.R.A.P. 9.09).","c":"W.R.A.P. 9.08(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (date the decision is rendered)","note":"File and serve within the period. Suspends proceedings (W.R.A.P. 9.09)."}},{"t":"Answer brief after rehearing granted","d":"15 days from entry of the order granting rehearing.","c":"W.R.A.P. 9.10","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order granting rehearing)","note":""}},{"t":"Mandate issues if no rehearing petition","d":"15 days from decision.\nAfter the mandate issues, the court cannot hear a costs motion (9.11(c)).","c":"W.R.A.P. 9.11(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (decision)","note":"After the mandate issues, the court cannot hear a costs motion (9.11(c))."}},{"t":"Motion for appellate costs or fees","d":"15 days from filing of the final written opinion or order.\nMay be extended for good cause or absent objection.","c":"W.R.A.P. 10.06","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the final written opinion or order)","note":"May be extended for good cause or absent objection."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of review (interlocutory/extraordinary)","d":"15 days from entry of the order from which relief is sought.\nFiled on paper with the docket fee. Does not stay the lower court unless ordered (13.06).","c":"W.R.A.P. 13.03(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order from which relief is sought)","note":"Filed on paper with the docket fee. Does not stay the lower court unless ordered (13.06)."}},{"t":"Response to petition for writ of review","d":"15 days from filing of the petition.","c":"W.R.A.P. 13.03(b)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Writ of review petition deemed denied","d":"40 days from filing of the petition.\nIf the reviewing court has not accepted review.","c":"W.R.A.P. 13.03(c)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition)","note":"If the reviewing court has not accepted review."}},{"t":"Petition for review of agency action (district court)","d":"30 days from service of the final agency decision (or of the denial of rehearing / decision after rehearing).\nApplies even where a statute sets a different time. Extension of up to 30 more days for excusable neglect (12.04(b)).","c":"W.R.A.P. 12.04(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the final agency decision)","note":"Applies even where a statute sets a different time. Extension of up to 30 more days for excusable neglect (12.04(b))."}},{"t":"Cross-petition for review of agency action","d":"15 days from filing of the first petition for review.","c":"W.R.A.P. 12.04(c)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first petition for review)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to certify agency-review case to Supreme Court","d":"30 days from filing of the petition for review.\n15 days to respond. The Supreme Court accepts or rejects within 30 days of receiving the certification order (12.09(d)).","c":"W.R.A.P. 12.09(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition for review)","note":"15 days to respond. The Supreme Court accepts or rejects within 30 days of receiving the certification order (12.09(d))."}},{"t":"Extra time after service by mail","d":"3 days from added to periods running from mail service.\nNot added for service through the e-filing system or for periods running from filing or a fixed date.","c":"W.R.A.P. 14.03","due":{"n":3,"from":"the trigger date (added to periods running from mail service)","note":"Not added for service through the e-filing system or for periods running from filing or a fixed date."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (civil or criminal)",30,"Entry of the appealable order or judgment","W.R.A.P. 2.01(a), 1.03(a)","Jurisdictional. File with the trial-court clerk, and serve a copy on the appellate clerk at the same time. On a showing of excusable neglect the trial court may extend the time, but only if the application is filed within 45 days of entry of the appealable order (W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1))."],["Application to extend time to appeal (excusable neglect)",45,"Entry of the appealable order","W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","Must be filed before day 45, with a proposed notice and the docket fee or an IFP motion. Deemed denied if not granted within 14 days of filing."],["Trial court order on extension application (else deemed denied)",14,"Filing of the extension application","W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(1)","If denied or deemed denied, the docket fee is refunded."],["Cross-appeal / other party’s notice of appeal",15,"Filing of the first notice of appeal","W.R.A.P. 2.01(a)(2)","Or within the original 30 days, whichever is later."],["Tolling motion: Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59 new trial or alter/amend",28,"Entry of judgment","W.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); W.R.A.P. 2.02(a)","The court cannot extend this time (W.R.C.P. 6(b)(2))."],["Post-trial motion deemed denied",90,"Filing of the Rule 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59 or 60(b) motion","W.R.C.P. 6(c)(4)","Unless continued by order within the 90 days, for no more than 60 more days. The 30-day appeal time then runs from the deemed denial."],["Notice of appeal after tolling motion",30,"Entry of the order deciding the last tolling motion, or its deemed denial","W.R.A.P. 2.02(b), 2.01(a)","A notice filed earlier takes effect on disposition. To challenge the ruling on the motion, file an amended notice within this time."],["Pay docket fee after IFP denial",30,"Entry of the order denying in forma pauperis status","W.R.A.P. 2.09(c)","If the fee is not paid, the appeal does not proceed."],["Appellee orders additional transcript (civil)",15,"Service of appellant’s partial-transcript designation","W.R.A.P. 3.02(b)","Or obtain a trial-court order directing the appellant to order those parts."],["Court reporter files transcript",60,"Filing of the notice of appeal","W.R.A.P. 2.06(a)","The trial court may extend it for good cause on a specific motion."],["Appellant’s brief",45,"Service of the notice that the appeal is docketed","W.R.A.P. 7.06(b)","File the record designation in the trial court at the same time (W.R.A.P. 3.05(b))."],["Appellee’s brief",45,"Service of the appellant’s brief","W.R.A.P. 7.06(c)","File any additional record designation at the same time."],["Reply brief",15,"Service of the appellee’s brief","W.R.A.P. 7.06(d), 7.03","Limited to new issues. Extensions are not considered absent extraordinary circumstances."],["Motion to extend briefing time",3,"Before the brief’s due date (working days)","W.R.A.P. 7.10(a)","Must certify good cause. A later motion must explain the delay."],["Amicus motion with proposed brief",11,"Filing of the principal brief of the party supported","W.R.A.P. 7.12(e)","An amicus supporting neither side: 11 days after the first party brief."],["Motion to move case from brief-only docket to argument",15,"Entry of the order assigning the brief-only docket","W.R.A.P. 8.01(c)","Requires good cause and is discretionary."],["Motion for extra oral argument time",15,"Before oral argument","W.R.A.P. 8.02(a)","Default is 30 minutes per side."],["Motion for oral argument by videoconference",20,"Before the scheduled argument date","W.R.A.P. 8.01(e)","Exceptional circumstances only. Later motions are allowed only for weather or unforeseen events."],["Response to an appellate motion",15,"Service of the motion","W.R.A.P. 16(b)","Applies to motions that may substantially affect disposition."],["Petition for rehearing",15,"Date the decision is rendered","W.R.A.P. 9.08(a)","File and serve within the period. Suspends proceedings (W.R.A.P. 9.09)."],["Answer brief after rehearing granted",15,"Entry of the order granting rehearing","W.R.A.P. 9.10",""],["Mandate issues if no rehearing petition",15,"Decision","W.R.A.P. 9.11(a)","After the mandate issues, the court cannot hear a costs motion (9.11(c))."],["Motion for appellate costs or fees",15,"Filing of the final written opinion or order","W.R.A.P. 10.06","May be extended for good cause or absent objection."],["Petition for writ of review (interlocutory/extraordinary)",15,"Entry of the order from which relief is sought","W.R.A.P. 13.03(a)","Filed on paper with the docket fee. Does not stay the lower court unless ordered (13.06)."],["Response to petition for writ of review",15,"Filing of the petition","W.R.A.P. 13.03(b)",""],["Writ of review petition deemed denied",40,"Filing of the petition","W.R.A.P. 13.03(c)","If the reviewing court has not accepted review."],["Petition for review of agency action (district court)",30,"Service of the final agency decision (or of the denial of rehearing / decision after rehearing)","W.R.A.P. 12.04(a)","Applies even where a statute sets a different time. Extension of up to 30 more days for excusable neglect (12.04(b))."],["Cross-petition for review of agency action",15,"Filing of the first petition for review","W.R.A.P. 12.04(c)",""],["Motion to certify agency-review case to Supreme Court",30,"Filing of the petition for review","W.R.A.P. 12.09(b)","15 days to respond. The Supreme Court accepts or rejects within 30 days of receiving the certification order (12.09(d))."],["Extra time after service by mail",3,"Added to periods running from mail service","W.R.A.P. 14.03","Not added for service through the e-filing system or for periods running from filing or a fixed date."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.wyocourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Wyoming Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope and purpose","s":"Govern procedure in all civil actions in Wyoming state courts, except actions in the chancery court (which has its own W.R.C.P.Ch.C.) and the exceptions in Rule 81. Construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action: the civil action.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint. Wyoming departure from FRCP 3: when an earlier action was dismissed under Rule 4(w) for failure to serve and a new action is filed, it is commenced by filing only if service is obtained within 90 days of the applicable statute of limitations; the comment ties this to Wyoming’s savings statute, W.S. 1-3-118.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Civil cover sheet [Repealed]","s":"Repealed (order of April 30, 2024, effective July 1, 2024). No civil cover sheet rule remains in the W.R.C.P.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"3.2","t":"Removal from district court to chancery court","s":"Wyoming-only rule. A district court case may be removed to the chancery court if all parties consent in writing within 60 days after service on the last defendant, or, if the case first becomes removable through an amended pleading, motion, order or other paper, by a notice of removal filed in chancery court within 60 days after the defendant receives that paper (again with all parties’ written consent). The case must meet W.S. 5-13-115 and the W.R.C.P.Ch.C. eligibility rules; the removing party promptly files a copy of the notice in district court, removal takes effect when the chancery court enters an Order of Removal, and district court filing fees are not refunded.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons and service of process [version effective September 21, 2026]","s":"Summons issued by the clerk for each defendant; served by any nonparty at least 18 or by a sheriff or U.S. marshal. Individuals are served personally, by leaving copies at the dwelling with a resident over 14, at the usual place of business with the employee in charge, or on an authorized agent; corporations through an officer, manager, general agent or agent for process (or, if none is found in the county, any agent or employee there, with the clerk mailing copies at least 20 days before default). The September 21, 2026 amendment replaced ‘service by publication’ with court-ordered ‘alternative service’ (Rule 4(k)-(o)): in the listed case types (mostly nonresident, property, probate, family, water and concealment cases) the party must file a motion and affidavit, and the court’s order sets the method, proof and completion; publication (four consecutive weeks) is allowed only if no other method is reasonably likely to give actual notice. Other limits: waiver request must allow at least 30 days (60 abroad) and a waiving defendant answers 60 days (90 abroad) after the request was sent; a notarized acceptance of service starts a 20-day answer period; service must be made within 90 days after filing or the court must dismiss without prejudice or set a deadline (Rule 4(w), not applicable to foreign service).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by hand, office or abode delivery, leaving with the clerk if no known address, mail (complete on mailing, sent simultaneously by electronic means unless the court orders otherwise), or any other means consented to in writing. Rule 26(a)(1)-(2) disclosures and discovery requests and responses are not filed until used, but a notice of discovery may be filed to show activity and avoid dismissal for lack of prosecution. Filing is through the e-filing system where the Wyoming Rules for Electronic Filing and Service require it; otherwise on paper or, for attorneys in courts without mandatory e-filing, by email (no initial pleadings or writ applications; 50-page cap; deemed filed on receipt, through 11:59:59 p.m. Mountain Time). Fax filing only in emergencies after calling the clerk, 10-page limit, with the original mailed within 24 hours; the clerk may refuse papers that obviously violate the Rules Governing Access to Case Records or the uniform rules.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Constitutional challenge to a statute","s":"When a party challenges the constitutionality of a Wyoming statute in a case where the state (or its officer, agency or employee) is not a party, the party must serve the attorney general with the pleading or motion raising the issue. Narrower than FRCP 5.1: no separate notice, certification or intervention deadline is stated.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Privacy protection for filings","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, all filings must comply with the Wyoming Rules Governing Access to Case Records (instead of the federal redaction list).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or (for filing) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible. The court may extend time before expiry for good cause or after expiry for excusable neglect, but not for Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e) and 60(b); the clerk may grant once, ex parte, an extension of up to 15 days to answer or move against a complaint or to respond to Rules 33, 34 or 36 discovery. Wyoming motion practice: motions are served at least 14 days before the hearing; responses are due at least 3 days before the hearing or within 20 days after service, whichever is earlier; replies at least 1 day before the hearing or within 15 days after the response, whichever is earlier; either side may request a hearing within 20 days. Post-judgment motions under Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59 and 60(b) not decided within 90 days after filing are deemed denied unless the court continues them by order (for no more than 60 more days). Add 3 days after service by mail or by delivery to the clerk (not for Rule 4(r) certified-mail process).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Lists the allowed pleadings (complaint, answer, answer to counterclaim, answer to crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if ordered). Motions must be in writing with particular grounds and relief sought; Wyoming requires that every Rule 12 and Rule 56 motion contain or be accompanied by a memorandum of points and authorities. Demurrers, pleas and exceptions are abolished.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Short and plain statement of jurisdiction, the claim, and the relief demanded; admissions, denials, and lack-of-knowledge responses as in FRCP 8. Unlike current FRCP 8(c), the affirmative-defense list still includes contributory negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, failure of consideration, and injury by fellow servant. Allegations other than damages are admitted if not denied in a required responsive pleading.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind may be alleged generally; denial of conditions precedent must be particular; special damages must be specifically stated. Wyoming adds 9(h): a municipal ordinance may be pleaded by its title or designation and the name of the municipality; there is no admiralty provision.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption with court name, title, file number and Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; exhibits are part of the pleading. Wyoming adds 10(d): every filed document must be on 8½ by 11 inch white paper, single-sided, unless the law requires filing an original of another size (such as a will).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","s":"Every paper is signed by an attorney of record (or unrepresented party) with address, email, telephone and attorney number. Signing certifies proper purpose and legal and factual support, as in FRCP 11. A sanctions motion must be made separately and served but not filed if the paper is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days (the safe harbor); no monetary sanction against a represented party for legal contentions; does not apply to discovery.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; time to answer","s":"Answer within 20 days after service, 30 days if served outside Wyoming, 30 days after the last day of publication, or 60 days after a waiver request was sent (90 if sent outside the United States). Answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim within 20 days; reply within 20 days after an order to reply. A Rule 12 motion moves the answer deadline to 14 days after notice of denial or after service of a more definite statement; a more definite statement must be supplied within 10 days after notice of the order; a motion to strike is due within 20 days when no response is allowed. Otherwise follows FRCP 12, including waiver of 12(b)(2)-(5) defenses. Note: 12(a)(1)(A)(iv) cross-references waiver ‘under Rule 4(d)’ although Wyoming’s waiver provision is Rule 4(u).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"12.1","t":"Change of venue (improper venue)","s":"Adopted effective October 6, 2025. A party’s motion to change venue must be made within 30 days after service of the complaint on the last party served; responses are due within 20 days after the motion is filed. The court may also act on its own after notice, with objections due within 20 days of the notice. If venue is improper the court must transfer to a district court where venue is proper and order the plaintiff to pay the refiling fee within 20 days, or the case is dismissed without prejudice; the record is not transferred until the plaintiff pays the receiving court’s filing fee.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Compulsory and permissive counterclaims and crossclaims as in FRCP 13. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the state, counties, municipalities or other political subdivisions or their officers and agencies. An omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment with leave.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who may be liable to it; leave of court is needed if filed more than 14 days after serving the original answer. Mirrors FRCP 14 without the admiralty provisions.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"One amendment as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise by written consent or leave, freely given. Response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back for a changed party must occur within the Rule 4(w) 90-day service period; notice to the state is satisfied by delivery to the Attorney General or the officer or agency.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The court may (not must) enter a scheduling order after consulting the parties; if entered it must limit time to join parties, amend, complete discovery and file motions, and it may be modified only for good cause with the judge’s consent. Wyoming adds exploring removal to chancery court as a conference purpose. Sanctions, including mandatory expenses, for failing to appear, being unprepared, or disobeying a pretrial order; the final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties; capacity; public officers","s":"Real party in interest; capacity of individuals is governed by Wyoming law and of corporations by the law of organization; partnerships and unincorporated associations may sue or be sued in their common name. Wyoming 17(d) (no federal equivalent): an unknown defendant may be sued under a fictitious name if the complaint states the true name could not be discovered, the summons says ‘real name unknown,’ and a copy is served personally.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims (such as a money claim plus a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Persons needed for complete relief or with an interest that would be impaired must be joined if feasible; if not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, using the listed factors. A claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and the reasons.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs and defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties or sever claims at any time on just terms.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead, and a defendant may seek interpleader by crossclaim or counterclaim.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Prerequisites and class types follow FRCP 23(a)-(b); certification, notice, judgment, settlement approval and class counsel follow the federal structure. Differences in the text: class counsel is Rule 23(f) and attorney’s fees are Rule 23(g), and there is no provision for a discretionary interlocutory appeal of a class-certification order (federal Rule 23(f)).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made on directors (or why none was made). Settlement or dismissal requires court approval and notice as the court orders.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Members may sue or be sued as representatives of an unincorporated association if they will fairly and adequately protect its interests; Rules 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention on timely motion, served under Rule 5 and accompanied by a pleading. The court must weigh undue delay or prejudice.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After a party’s death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action by or against the decedent must be dismissed. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers; substitution may occur before or after judgment.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","s":"Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 30 days after the answer is due (as extended by a Rule 12(b) dispositive motion), or within 30 days after a later-joined party is served; the list of exempt proceedings includes Title 14 (juvenile) cases, probate, administrative-record review, and others, and divorce and unmarried custody/support cases have their own detailed disclosures (26(a)(1.1)-(1.2)). Expert disclosures, with a signed report or a disclosure signed by counsel for retained experts, are due at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s); pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial with objections 14 days later. No discovery from any source until the initial-disclosure period has expired and the requesting party has made its own disclosures. There is no mandatory Rule 26(f) meeting: the court holds a discovery conference on a party’s motion (objections due within 14 days). A motion for protective order directed to a deposition must be filed at least 3 days before it, and filing the motion stays the deposition or discovery until ruling (26(c)(4)). Scope is proportionality-based as in FRCP 26(b)(1).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Before suit, a verified petition is filed in the district court where an expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing; the court may appoint an attorney for unserved parties. Depositions may also be perpetuated pending appeal on motion.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken; interstate subpoenas","s":"Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized by Wyoming, federal or local law, or a court-appointed person; foreign depositions by treaty, letter of request, notice or commission. Wyoming 28(c) (interstate discovery): to subpoena a witness in Wyoming for an out-of-state case, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk of court in the county where discovery will occur; the clerk issues a Wyoming subpoena served under Rule 45, and doing so is not an appearance in Wyoming courts. Disqualifies officers who are relatives, employees, or attorneys of a party or financially interested.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery cutoff, a motion hearing, or trial.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Leave is required for more than 10 depositions per side, a second deposition of the same person, a deposition before the Rule 26(d) time (unless the notice certifies the deponent will leave Wyoming), or a prisoner. One day of 7 hours unless stipulated or ordered; objections concise and nonsuggestive; instructions not to answer only for privilege, court limits, or a 30(d)(3) motion. The deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign changes if requested before the deposition ends; 30(b)(6) organizational depositions as in federal practice.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"30.1","t":"Remote depositions","s":"Wyoming-only protocol (effective October 1, 2024) for depositions where participants are not in the same place, unless stipulated or ordered otherwise. The noticing party must identify the remote vendor and give access instructions at least 7 days before; the parties must meet and confer on logistics and run a technology test at least 24 hours before; an attorney who will sit in the same room as the witness must give 24 hours’ notice. Microphones and video stay on; no private chat or other applications; no unrecorded communications with the witness on the record; exhibits are shared by sealed envelope or electronically; technical glitches do not waive objections, and a compliant remote deposition is usable at trial like an in-person video deposition.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Same 10-deposition and leave limits as Rule 30. Cross-questions are served within 14 days after the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may change these times.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court","s":"Deposition use at trial or hearing follows FRCP 32, with a Wyoming difference in the unavailability test: a witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is ‘absent from the state’ (not the federal 100-mile test), dead, unable to attend, or could not be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition taken on less than 14 days’ notice cannot be used against a party with a pending protective-order motion; objections to written-question form must be served within the time for responsive questions (7 days for recross).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"No more than 25 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, without stipulation or leave. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service; objections must be specific or are waived; answers are under oath, and business records may be produced instead when the burden is equal.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and tangible things; entry onto land","s":"Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify ESI form. Written response due within 30 days after service; objections must state reasons and say whether responsive material is being withheld. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause, the court may order a party (or a person in its custody or legal control) whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the order sets time, place, manner and scope. Report exchange and privilege waiver as in FRCP 35(b).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service. Denials must be specific; lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. No numerical limit in the rule itself, but Rule 26(b)(2)(A) lets the court limit the number. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to disclose or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel require a good-faith conferral certification; expenses follow the result. Sanctions for disobeying discovery orders (including Rule 26(f) discovery-conference orders), automatic exclusion of undisclosed information or witnesses unless substantially justified or harmless, expenses for failing to admit, sanctions for failing to attend one’s own deposition or respond to discovery, and the federal-style ESI-loss rule in 37(e).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Right to jury trial; demand","s":"Issues of fact in actions for money only or specific real or personal property are tried to a jury unless waived; other fact issues go to the court. Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it. Wyoming requires a jury fee deposit with the demand: in district court $50 for a 6-person jury or $150 for a 12-person jury; in circuit court $50. The fee is taxed as costs against the losing party; failure to demand properly waives a jury; other parties may demand additional issues within 14 days.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the court finds no jury right, or a party fails to appear and those present consent. The court may use an advisory jury, or with consent a binding jury, except where a statute requires a nonjury trial against the State.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"39.1","t":"Juror note taking; juror notebooks","s":"At the start of civil trials the court must tell jurors they may take notes and supply materials; notes stay in the courthouse, are collected daily, and are collected and promptly destroyed after deliberations end. The court may provide identical juror notebooks.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"39.2","t":"Juror questionnaires","s":"In appropriate cases the court may use case-specific questionnaires; counsel should confer on the questions, and the court rules on disputed questions and states its reasons on the record.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"39.3","t":"Copies of instructions for jurors","s":"Each juror receives a personal copy of all written instructions read to the jury, which may be kept during recesses and deliberations but not taken out of the courthouse.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"39.4","t":"Juror questions for witnesses","s":"The court must tell jurors at the start of civil trials that they may submit written questions for witnesses after counsel finish. The court reviews questions with counsel out of the jury’s hearing, rules on objections on the record, and allows rebuttal evidence if needed to prevent unfair prejudice.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment for trial; ADR","s":"The court sets cases for trial on its own, on request, or as it finds expedient, with statutory priorities. For nonbinding ADR, the court may (and on any party’s request must) assign a settlement conference or mediation to another active judge, a retired judge or justice, or another qualified person, or the parties may choose one; recommended mediation procedures are listed, the neutral’s bill is due within 30 days of receipt, and ADR does not suspend deadlines or hearings.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"40.1","t":"Transfer of trial and change of judge","s":"Transfer of trial to another county for local prejudice or witness convenience: move within 15 days after the last pleading is filed; the movant pays transfer costs within 14 days. Peremptory disqualification (Wyoming-specific; no federal equivalent): a party may disqualify the assigned district judge without cause, once per case and against only one judge. The plaintiff must move within 14 days after the clerk’s notice of assignment (which the clerk must enter within 5 days after the complaint is filed) or after a reassignment order, whichever is later; the defendant must move by the time of its first responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion, or 14 days after a reassignment order, whichever is later. Parties added after the first responsive pleading cannot use it, and new claims or motions do not revive it; it does not apply in criminal or juvenile cases. Disqualification for cause (prior counsel, interest, kinship, material witness, bias) is by motion with affidavits when the grounds become known; rulings are not separately appealable but may be raised on appeal from the final judgment.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions [version effective September 21, 2026]","s":"Plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal is by court order, and a counterclaim pleaded before service of the plaintiff’s motion stays pending for independent adjudication. New 41(a)(3) (September 21, 2026): a voluntary dismissal is not a ‘failure otherwise than upon the merits’ under W.S. 1-3-118, so the savings statute does not rescue a voluntarily dismissed claim. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply operates on the merits unless the order says otherwise; the court may also dismiss without prejudice, after notice, cases not prosecuted with due diligence (see U.R.D.C. 203).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions with a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial or consolidated; separate trials may be ordered for convenience, prejudice or economy, preserving any jury right.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a rule or statute provides otherwise; remote contemporaneous testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances with safeguards. Affirmation may replace oath; motions may be heard on affidavits or testimony; the court may appoint and tax the cost of an interpreter.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Determining foreign law","s":"A party intending to raise foreign-country law must give written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk issues signed blank subpoenas, and attorneys may issue them as officers of the court; notice to other parties is required before serving a document subpoena. Served anywhere in Wyoming by a sheriff or any nonparty adult, with one day’s fee and mileage tendered; a witness from another county also gets the state-employee per diem afterward. Place limits differ from federal: trial subpoenas can require attendance anywhere in the state; a deposition witness can be required to attend only in the county of residence, employment, or regular business (a nonresident, in the county where served). Written objections to a document subpoena are due within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds. No prejudice if there was no opportunity to object.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Selecting jurors","s":"Jurors answer qualification questions in writing under penalty of perjury. Attorneys (or pro se parties) conduct voir dire under the judge’s control, with listed prohibitions (no preconditioning, no questions on the law or the pleadings, no individual questions that could be asked of the group, no hypothetical-verdict questions) but may preview evidence in a nonargumentative way. Each party has 3 peremptory challenges (several plaintiffs or defendants may be treated as one party); up to 6 alternates may be seated.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict; polling","s":"A jury of at least 6 and no more than 12; the verdict must be unanimous and returned by at least 6 jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise; polling on request.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdict; general verdict with questions","s":"Special verdicts and general verdicts with written questions as in FRCP 49; issues not submitted are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; renewed motion","s":"JMOL may be moved for any time before submission. A renewed motion (with or without an alternative new-trial request) must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after discharge if the issue was not decided by verdict; a losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due within 28 days after entry. Undecided post-trial motions are deemed denied under Rule 6(c)(4) after 90 days.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"Requests at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered; the court must disclose proposed instructions and rulings before instructing and arguments, and objections must be made on the record, stating distinctly the matter and grounds. Instructions are reduced to writing, numbered, delivered to the jury, and made part of the record.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; reserved questions","s":"Different from FRCP 52(a): in a bench trial the court need not state findings except generally, unless a party requests written special findings before evidence is introduced; findings are unnecessary on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend or add findings is due within 28 days after entry of judgment. Judgment on partial findings as in federal practice. Wyoming 52(d): a court may reserve an important and difficult constitutional question for the Wyoming Supreme Court only after making all necessary findings and conclusions.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Wyoming’s master rule differs from current FRCP 53: reference is the exception (complicated issues in jury cases; exceptional conditions in nonjury cases except accounts and difficult damages). The master sets the first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference; in nonjury actions the court accepts findings unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 14 days after notice of its filing.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs; attorney’s fees","s":"A decision or opinion letter is not a judgment. Rule 54(b) certification requires an express finding of no just reason for delay. Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party on a motion filed no later than 21 days after entry of judgment (Wyoming requires a motion and has a deadline for costs, unlike the federal clerk-taxation process). Attorney’s fee motions are also due within 21 days after entry unless a statute or order provides otherwise, and must state the grounds and amount.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"The clerk enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend, and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a nonappearing, competent adult. Otherwise the court enters judgment; a party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. Defaults are set aside for good cause; default judgments under Rule 60(b); judgment against the state requires evidence satisfying the court.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A party may move at any time unless an order sets a different time; the standard (no genuine dispute of material fact) and procedures follow FRCP 56, and the court should state on the record its reasons for granting or denying. Deadlines to respond are set by Rule 6(c) (response 20 days after service or 3 days before the hearing, whichever is earlier), and Rule 7(b)(2) requires a memorandum of points and authorities. See Rule 56.1 for the required statement of facts.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"56.1","t":"Summary judgment: statement of material facts","s":"Wyoming-only rule. The movant must attach a separate, short, concise statement of material facts claimed to be undisputed, and the opponent must attach a separate statement of facts claimed to be genuinely disputed; both must include pinpoint citations to the record.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgment","s":"These rules govern statutory declaratory-judgment actions; Rules 38-39 govern jury demands; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief; the court may order a speedy hearing.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entering judgment","s":"If parties cannot agree on the form, the proposed judgment or order is presented to the court and served within 14 days after the decision is made known; objections, with an alternate form, are due within 5 days after service. Every judgment must be on a separate document naming all parties in the caption, and a judgment is entered when the signed form is filed with the clerk. A timely Rule 54(d)(2) fee motion can be given the same effect as a Rule 59 motion under W.R.A.P. 2.02(a) if the court so orders before a notice of appeal takes effect.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"Wyoming lists 8 specific grounds for a new trial (irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, excessive damages from passion or prejudice, error in the amount, insufficient evidence or contrary to law, newly discovered evidence, and error of law at trial). Motions for new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment; opposing affidavits within 14 days (extendable up to 21 days); the court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. Undecided motions are deemed denied after 90 days (Rule 6(c)(4)).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from a judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected anytime, but only with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed and pending. Rule 60(b) grounds mirror the federal rule; motions must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than 1 year after entry. The motion does not affect finality; independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief are preserved.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded and are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution until 14 days after entry of judgment (the federal automatic stay is 30 days), except injunctions and receiverships, which are not stayed unless ordered. The court may stay execution pending Rule 50, 52(b), 59 or 60 motions on terms. On appeal, a stay is obtained by a supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it, subject to the Wyoming Rules of Appellate Procedure; no bond is required of the State, its officers or agencies.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"62.1","t":"Indicative ruling while an appeal is pending","s":"If an appeal is docketed and pending, the trial court may defer, deny, or state it would grant the motion (or that it raises a substantial issue); the movant must promptly notify the appellate clerk, and the trial court may decide the motion on remand.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record; at a party’s request the successor must recall material, disputed witnesses in a hearing or bench trial. After verdict or findings, another judge in the district or a judge or justice designated by the Supreme Court may perform the remaining duties, or grant a new trial if unable to do so.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizing a person or property","s":"All statutory remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available at the start of and during the action.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"Preliminary injunctions require notice; a TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney certification, expires in no more than 14 days unless extended for a like period, and may be challenged on 2 days’ notice. Security in an amount the court considers proper is required before any preliminary injunction or TRO. The rule does not apply to divorce, alimony, separate maintenance, or child-custody suits.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Proceedings against a surety","s":"A surety on a bond or undertaking submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent for service; liability may be enforced by motion without a separate action.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order; administration of estates by receivers follows prior Wyoming practice, and otherwise these rules apply.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"With leave and on notice, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute; funds go into an interest-bearing account or court-approved instrument and are withdrawn by court order or written stipulation. Before disbursement the requesting party must supply payee information in a form that complies with the redaction rules.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of settlement or judgment","s":"Broader than FRCP 68: any party (not only a defending party) may serve an offer of settlement or judgment, at any time more than 60 days after service of the complaint and at least 28 days before trial. Acceptance within 14 days; an unaccepted offer is withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer; ‘costs’ do not include attorney’s fees. After liability is determined, an offer may be made at least 14 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise; the judgment creditor (or a successor of record) may take discovery in aid of execution under these rules.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","s":"If a party fails to perform a required act, the court may appoint another to do it at that party’s expense, divest and vest title to property within the district, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"Orders granting relief to, or enforceable against, a nonparty are enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"Wyoming eminent-domain procedure, keyed to W.S. 1-26-501 et seq. The complaint must describe the authority, use, necessity and property and the pre-suit negotiation efforts required by statute; an answer is needed only to contest the right to take or necessity and is due 5 days before the hearing. Show-cause hearings on immediate entry and hearings on the condemnation complaint are held no sooner than 15 days after service. Compensation is set informally by the judge (if the amount in dispute is under $20,000 or the gap between offer and demand is under $5,000 and informal procedure is requested) or by three disinterested county-resident appraisers; after an informal judgment any party may demand a formal court or jury trial within 30 days.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"District courts and clerks; notice of orders and judgments","s":"District courts are always open for filing; trials are in open court, and nonex parte hearings outside the county need the consent of all nondefaulted parties. The clerk must serve every nondefaulted party with each order or judgment immediately on entry (by mail, hand, clerk’s box, or electronically), but lack of notice does not extend the time to appeal except as the appellate rules allow. (There are no W.R.C.P. Rules 72-76; Wyoming has no magistrate-judge or appeal-to-district-court rules in this set.)","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Hearing motions; decision on briefs","s":"A court may set regular times for motion hearings and may decide motions on briefs without oral hearing.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps the books and records required by statute and any others required by the Supreme Court or the district judge.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic transcript as evidence","s":"When previously reported testimony is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability in general","s":"Statutes inconsistent with these rules do not apply, except that in special statutory proceedings a rule does not apply where clearly inapplicable, and where a statute creating a special proceeding sets form, content, or timing of papers, either the statute or these rules may be followed.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Uniform rules; judge’s directives","s":"A court conference may adopt uniform rules with Supreme Court approval, and no court may adopt procedural rules applicable only in that court (no individual local rules). A judge may regulate practice consistently with law, but no sanction may be imposed for violating a requirement not in statute, state rules, or the uniform rules unless the party had actual notice of it in the case.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"No forms are provided with these rules (an Appendix of Forms is published separately on the court website).","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"Cited as the Wyoming Rules of Civil Procedure, W.R.C.P.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective dates","s":"The current rules took effect March 1, 2017, for new and pending cases unless infeasible or unjust. Amendments take effect on dates the Supreme Court sets, or, if none is set, 60 days after publication in the Pacific Reporter advance sheets.","u":"https://www.wyocourts.gov/app/uploads/2025/01/WY-Rules-of-Civ.-Procedure-Eff.-9-21-26.pdf"}]});
